<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Cambodia</h1><p class="st-meta">1989 CE – 1993 CE · capital Phnom Penh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The renamed and reformed successor of the People's Republic of Kampuchea, which governed Cambodia during the peace process of 1989 to 1993.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In April 1989 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peoples-republic-of-kampuchea">People's Republic of Kampuchea</a> renamed itself the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia">Cambodia</a>, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> as the state religion, reintroduced private property and adopted a new flag, as Vietnamese troops completed their withdrawal in September 1989.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ruling party, renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">Cambodian People's Party</a> in 1991, governed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heng-samrin">Heng Samrin</a> and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hun-sen">Hun Sen</a> while negotiating with the resistance factions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-agreements">Paris Peace Agreements</a> of October 1991 set up a Supreme National Council chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Sihanouk</a> and placed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia">Cambodia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-transitional-authority-in-cambodia">United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia</a>, UNTAC, which oversaw elections.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The UN-run elections of May 1993 were won by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-ranariddh">Norodom Ranariddh's</a> royalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funcinpec">FUNCINPEC</a> party, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodian-peoples-party">CPP</a> second. A new constitution restored the monarchy, and the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia">Cambodia</a> gave way to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in September 1993.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1989 CE – 1993 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:99.28%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1989 CE – 1993 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phnom Penh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heng Samrin, Chea Sim, Hun Sen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cambodia">Cambodia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>SOC</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=state-of-cambodia" aria-label="State of Cambodia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7566 1746 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Cambodia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7886 2002L7886 2002M7886 2002L7886 2002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7886 2002h0M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7886 2002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7886 2002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Phnom Penh. <a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=state-of-cambodia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
